{"name":"Black Howl","hex":"#202030","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"black","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Absorbing all light and reflecting none; dark and hueless.","Without light."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour/color perceived in the absence of light, but also when no light is reflected, but rather absorbed.","A black dye or pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black"},{"term":"howl","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The protracted, mournful cry of a dog, wolf or other canid; also of other animals.","Any similar sound."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To utter a loud, protracted, mournful sound or cry, as dogs and wolves often do.","To utter a sound expressive of pain or distress; to cry aloud and mournfully; to lament; to wail."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/howl"}],"entries":[{"title":"Howl (Black Rebel Motorcycle Club album)","blocks":[{"text":"Howl is the third studio album by American rock band Black Rebel Motorcycle Club. It was released on August 22, 2005 in the UK, August 23 in the US, and September 21 in Japan. The record was released in the UK and Europe by The Echo Label and by RCA in the US, Australia, Japan, and the rest of the world. The title of the record is a direct reference to Allen Ginsberg's poem \"Howl\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Brmc_howl.jpg/330px-Brmc_howl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Howl_(Black_Rebel_Motorcycle_Club_album)","wikidata":"Q10575"},{"title":"Howl (poem)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Howl\", also known as \"Howl for Carl Solomon\", is a poem written by Allen Ginsberg in 1954–1955 and published in his 1956 collection, Howl and Other Poems. The poem is dedicated to Carl Solomon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Howl_and_Other_Poems_%28first_edition%29.jpg/330px-Howl_and_Other_Poems_%28first_edition%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Howl_(poem)","wikidata":"Q1545259"},{"title":"Black","blocks":[{"text":"Black is a color that results from the absence or complete absorption of visible light. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like white and grey. It is often used symbolically or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the Age of Enlightenment, and night versus day. Since the Middle Ages, black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png/330px-Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black","wikidata":"Q23445"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Yleisradio-hallintoneuvosto-1969.jpg/500px-Yleisradio-hallintoneuvosto-1969.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Yleisradio-hallintoneuvosto-1969.jpg/1280px-Yleisradio-hallintoneuvosto-1969.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Yleisradio-hallintoneuvosto-1969.jpg/500px-Yleisradio-hallintoneuvosto-1969.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The administrative board of Yleisradio getting ready to discuss the broadcast of “Howl” in December 1969.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYleisradio-hallintoneuvosto-1969.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Yellowstone-black-bear-07895.jpg/500px-Y